{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795713177,"nonce":"YOuKoTUwFMMruSj8glbDL39PfzQ=","title":"fem.eval of a weak term with u.t returns the x-derivative term instead of zero or an error","message":"`fem.eval(term, u)` reads a weak term back at a state vector `u`. When the term contains a time derivative `u.t`, the result is not zero and is not refused: it equals the same term with `u.x` in place of `u.t`.\n\n**What goes wrong.** `fem.eval` assembles the free residual at t = 0 from a single state vector, which carries no time derivative. The documentation says nothing about `u.t`, so the sensible reading is \"zero\" (or a clear error). Observed on a 2-D transient heat form `u.t*v + u.x*v.x + u.y*v.y` (unit square, h = 0.2, P1): `fem.eval(ui.t*vi, w)` equals `fem.eval(ui.x*vi, w)` to the last bit for every w tried (max difference 0.0 for w = x, x^2, 1+x; it is zero only when w does not vary in x, e.g. y or a constant). For a smooth test vector the spurious part is 18 % of the norm of the diffusion part (|T|/|S| = 0.116 for w = sin(3x)cos(2y)+0.3), so `fem.eval(ui.t*vi + ui.x*vi.x + ui.y*vi.y, w)` differs from the stiffness-only readout by 11.6 % in the relative 2-norm. The size depends on how much w varies in x, so it can look like a small, plausible discrepancy (in one case it appeared as about 0.5 % of the stiffness part) and goes unnoticed.\n\n**Reproduce.**\n\n    d = jno.domain(box(0,0,1,1), mesh_size=0.2, time=(0.0, 0.5, 51))\n    u, v = d.fem_symbols(); xi, yi, ti = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)\n    ui, vi = u.bind(x=xi, y=yi, t=ti), v.bind(x=xi, y=yi, t=ti)\n    fem = jno.fem([ui.t*vi + ui.x*vi.x + ui.y*vi.y, ...boundary and initial terms...])\n    w = np.sin(3*X) * np.cos(2*Y) + 0.3          # a vector on the nodes\n    fem.eval(ui.t*vi, w)  -\u003e  equals fem.eval(ui.x*vi, w), expected 0 (or an error)\n\nChecked on main (the same on a newer branch). I did not trace the cause; it looks like the time slot of the derivative being read as the first spatial axis in the weak-term path.\n\n**Expected.** Either zero, with the documented rule \"the state has no time dependence, u.t = 0\", or a ValueError telling the caller to use `fem.M @ du_dt` for the transient part. Silent substitution of another derivative is the one outcome that is wrong in every case.\n\n**Where.** `jno/_fem.py`: `FEM.eval` and `_eval_at` (the weak branch, `_term_residual_factory(...)`), and the native kernel's handling of a time derivative in a steady-evaluated term.\n\n**Workaround.** Keep `u.t` out of terms handed to `fem.eval`; read the mass part from `fem.M`.\n\n**Direction.** Make the weak path raise on a time-derivative node unless the problem is read at a state with a stated rate, and add a test that `eval(u.t * v, w)` is zero or raises.","files":null}]}